Subject: Report a C++ deprecation through `ParseCallbacks`

bindgen has no deprecation handling at all: nothing reads
`clang_getCursorAvailability`, and `[[deprecated]]` reaches the parser only as
`CXCursor_UnexposedAttr`, which flips one opaque boolean and is thrown away.
So neither the marker nor the message it carries appears anywhere in bindgen's
output or in anything it tells a consumer.

That matters to a consumer which emits code of its own, in two directions.
Rust wants `#[deprecated]` on the binding, which is where a caller should hear
about it. C++ generated alongside - a shim which calls the function - names
the deprecated declaration and so draws `-Wdeprecated-declarations`, whether
or not anything calls the binding; a project which builds with `-Werror` then
cannot bind the function at all. `[[deprecated]]` is encoded in no mangled
name, so there is no back channel to recover it from either.

`denote_deprecation` reports it, for functions and methods. Purely additive:
no generated code changes.

The message is the fiddly part. `clang_getCursorAvailability` says whether the
declaration is deprecated - by `[[deprecated]]`, by
`__attribute__((deprecated))` or by `__declspec(deprecated)` - and says nothing
about what it was written with. `clang_getCursorPlatformAvailability` is the
call which hands back the message, and it is asked here for no platform
entries: a plain deprecation names no platform, and entries would only have to
be freed again. Both functions are in libclang's stable C API and are already
linked in.

Each answers for the declaration it is asked about and no other. A member of a
class or a namespace which is itself `[[deprecated]]` reports as available,
even though naming the member is what draws the warning, so an enclosing
deprecation is not reported here and would have to be looked for on the
enclosing declaration.

`Deprecation` is a struct holding one optional message rather than the message
alone, so that the other things a deprecation carries - a replacement, a
version, the platforms an availability attribute names - have somewhere to go
without changing the callback's signature again.
